message: "Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its LaTeX source and its verification scripts."
type: software
title: "The price of precision: usable entanglement in nonlocal games and the geometry of groups"
version: 1.1.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23697345"
abstract: "In a nonlocal game of value one, entanglement demand at loss $\\varepsilon$ is the infimum of the one-way distillable entanglement of an original strategy state losing at most $\\varepsilon$. For synchronous games with tested diagonals and constraint-variable consistency games, this demand diverges exactly when no finite-dimensional strategy is perfect. Every good enough mixed strategy yields any fixed number of Bell pairs at any fixed fidelity below one by local isometries. Under a promise, a sufficient loss is computable for rational synchronous or binary constraint games, but bounded demand is undecidable among games of value one. Agreement games have the same demand in both directions up to a factor three in loss. A sign relation and an even dilation, mirrored by both players, force a logarithmic lower bound. Theorem 1.1 of our companion \"The uncomputable price of precision\" (10.5281/zenodo.23736188) realizes every positive $\\Delta^0_2$ logarithmic coefficient with a fixed game on the original mixed state. We classify the positive pure original-state entropy-coefficient spectrum as the positive $\\Delta^0_2$ reals; the full usable spectrum remains open. The least usable entanglement compatible with $M$ approximate Pauli pairs at pairwise error $\\kappa\\le1/100$ is of order $\\min\\{M,\\kappa^{-2}\\}$. Group geometry gives logarithmic laws, an almost inverse-loss law, and a point-Pauli game with demand between $\\varepsilon^{-\\beta}$ and $\\varepsilon^{-1/4}$, where $\\beta=0.0734595360384268649\\ldots$. Its lower bound uses explicit far-commutator fillings; a magnetic-cycle estimate obstructs improving the fixed-holonomy subdivision alone. Under exact group relations, demand counts qubits addressable by short words, with polynomial and exponential laws. Conditional on attainment and tail bounds, the $I_{3322}$ optimum needs bounded usable entanglement."
